  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nBreezy south to southwest winds and lots of sunshine will again\nhelp push temperatures to near record to record values in the \nlower to middle 90s. With a little higher humidity than \nyesterday, heat index values will generally be 3-6 degrees above\nthe air temperatures. Southwest winds around 5mph will continue\ninto tonight, and this will only allow lows to drop to around \n70 degrees, resulting in some record warm lows. Temperatures \nwill remain above normal through Thursday, but clouds and \nprecipitation will bring readings down several degrees.\n\nModels continue to sink a frontal boundary southward toward, then \ninto the PAH forecast area (fa) tonight into Wednesday, lingering \nacross our region into early Thursday. 20-35% chances of \nshowers and some storms will spread into southern Illinois and \nsoutheast Missouri late tonight, with 60-80% chances across the \nentire PAH forecast area Wednesday. 30-70% chances will continue\ninto Wednesday night and Thursday, and the best chances will be\nalong and north of the boundary, so this will focus the highest\nchances across northern portions of the PAH fa. Low pressure \nlifting out of the southern Plains into the Great Lakes region \nwill lift the boundary north late Thursday, then it will drag a\ncold front across the middle Mississippi and lower Ohio valleys\nThursday night. Lesser precipitation chances are expected with \nthe cold front, with just 20-40% chances of showers and isolated\nthunderstorms Thursday night across all but southern portions \nof west Kentucky. \n\nIt will be dry, very breezy and much cooler Friday as we see \nnorthwest winds increasing to 15-25mph with gusts near 30mph. \nHigh temperatures will be around 5 degrees below normal in the \nmiddle 60s to around 70 degrees. The coolest period will be \nSaturday and Saturday night, with highs in the 60 to 65 degree \nrange, and lows of 35 to 40 degrees. With calm winds, patchy \nfrost is not out of the question Saturday night. On Sunday, a \nreturn of southerly winds will begin a warming trend, with near \nseasonable temperatures Sunday, and a little above normal\nreadings Monday.",
